DLP Lab 0.2.16 for Debian 13; Debian 12 requires a separate native build
===================================================

Unified operations guide: docs/OPERATIONS.md in the project repository.
The versioned sections below also retain implementation history.

The package contains administrator-owned code and a system files service.
First installation can ask for the desktop account and Telegram path through
debconf. If configuration is declined, no account is selected; the files service
is skipped until /etc/dlp-lab/system-gateway.conf exists. The interactive
dlp-lab-configure --wizard can configure it afterwards.

Fresh installation:
  sudo apt install ./dlp-lab_0.2.16_amd64.deb
  sudo dlp-lab-configure --user YOUR_USER --telegram /absolute/path/to/Telegram --enable
  sudo systemctl status dlp-lab-files.service
  sudo journalctl -u dlp-lab-files.service -n 30
Readiness requires monitor_started, not merely active/running.

Configure accepts only a non-root account and a root-owned, protected executable.
Host settings are generated, never sourced as shell. To replace them, stop the
service and use --replace (backs up both files in /var/lib/dlp-lab-package;
resets exclusions). Edit exclusions as root in system-gateway.conf and restart.
Policy is a dpkg conffile in /etc/dlp-lab/system/policy.yaml: local edits survive
upgrades using the normal dpkg conffile mechanism. The adjacent processes.yaml
is generated by configure, not overwritten by upgrades.

Migration from the laboratory:
  1. Stop the user files service: scripts/dlp agent stop files
  2. Install the package, then configure the user/Telegram path as above.
  3. Revoke the laboratory NOPASSWD gateway rule as administrator before treating
     the deployment as administrator-managed. Preserve a root-only backup of
     /etc/sudoers.d/dlp-lab-fanotify outside /etc/sudoers.d. Check sudo visudo -c.
  4. Keep the user files service stopped; do not run the legacy updater on the
     packaged installation. Both gateways share /etc/dlp-lab/gateway.lock to
     reject concurrent operation. The package never edits /usr/local or the
     original laboratory configuration.

Control:
  sudo systemctl stop/start/restart dlp-lab-files.service
  sudo systemctl disable --now dlp-lab-files.service
  sudo journalctl -u dlp-lab-files.service
The old `dlp-agent agent` lifecycle is for laboratory user units; for packaged
file protection use the systemctl commands above. Optional text-field/local
storage tools remain explicit user-session commands, never root jobs. The
web dashboard's user-unit controls are not a system-service administration UI.

Updates stop and restart the configured system service; there is a protection
gap during update/restart. Administrator disabling/masking is respected by the
Debian service helpers. Removal stops the service and preserves configuration;
purge removes the policy and generated package host settings. Previous config
backups, unrelated files, the laboratory lock/config and all user data are kept.

Security and scope:
  Only the native broker runs as root. The analyzer drops to the configured user,
  with no capabilities and NoNewPrivs; it receives selected read-only FDs.
  Package code does not use the user's PYTHONPATH, cwd or user-site packages.
  The service deliberately uses the host mount view (no PrivateTmp/ProtectHome)
  to cover host /tmp and /home. Local mount inventory is a startup snapshot;
  new mounts require restart. No network/FUSE/OCR coverage is claimed.
  A user can still kill their own analyzer; systemd restarts after broker failure.
  This is recovery, not tamper resistance. Root can always disable protection.
  Notifications require an active user session bus; journal works without login.
  Opening a file is not proof of sending it. BLOCK and confirmed DENY are distinct.
  ML is disabled by default; its packaged model uses synthetic research data.

Dependencies are installed by apt, not pip and not vendored. The Python crypto dependency also supports the offline Telegram-local reader. No Telegram binary/profile,
training corpus, private export, local log, or build fixture is included.

Version 0.2.1: interactive first install
--------------------------------------
  sudo apt install ./dlp-lab_0.2.16_amd64.deb
The installer asks whether to configure, for the desktop login name, and for
Telegram's absolute executable path. Stop the laboratory files monitor first.
With valid answers it creates host settings and starts the system service;
boot activation is handled by the systemd Debian helpers. Existing host settings
are not overwritten on upgrade. An empty username or choosing No leaves it
unconfigured. Noninteractive installers must preseed debconf answers or run
sudo dlp-lab-configure afterwards. No username is silently guessed.
If setup fails (for example a running gateway holds the lock), apt reports an
installation error. Resolve the cause and run sudo dpkg --configure dlp-lab.
Check the journal for monitor_started; a successful apt exit alone is not proof
of active protection. Text-field monitoring is not automatically started.

Manual operation after package installation
-------------------------------------------
  sudo systemctl stop dlp-lab-files.service
  sudo dlp-lab-run-files
This runs the fixed packaged broker in the terminal, using the same system
policy and host configuration. Ctrl+C stops it. No systemd user unit is needed.
The shared gateway lock rejects concurrent system/manual/laboratory monitors.
To return to automatic operation:
  sudo systemctl start dlp-lab-files.service
To keep future boots manual as well:
  sudo systemctl disable --now dlp-lab-files.service
To restore automatic startup:
  sudo systemctl enable --now dlp-lab-files.service

Unprivileged manual analysis, with no monitor running, remains available:
  dlp-agent analyze --text 'ordinary test message'
For an explicitly prepared visible Telegram test draft, as the desktop user:
  dlp-agent text-monitor --marker DLP_TEST --seconds 120
This observes a chosen draft and warns; it does not veto sending. Notifications
need the user's graphical session; file enforcement itself runs without login.
The source-tree ./scripts/dlp commands are retained for development. Do not run
the laboratory file monitor alongside the packaged file monitor.

Build and validation (0.2.2)
----------------------------
Build with scripts/build-deb.sh after installing debian/control Build-Depends.
For this development host, --local-deps explicitly skips dpkg's Build-Depends
check and runs tests with the existing .deps; .deps is never shipped.
Artifacts and logs stay in build/. No maintainer identity is invented: replace
the placeholder Maintainer address before an official repository submission.

scripts/test-deb-lifecycle.sh PACKAGE.deb PYTHON3-YAML.deb [PREVIOUS.deb]
checks real dpkg install/configure/upgrade/remove/purge in a rootless chroot,
using copied host runtime and package database. It uses --no-triggers and no
running systemd. This verifies package scripts and configuration preservation,
not fanotify, live restart, reboot, or a clean base distribution dependency set.
The test also checks that caller PYTHONPATH cannot replace packaged imports.
Python bytecode is cleaned on removal; debconf answers are removed on purge.


Optional Signal Desktop (0.2.3)
-----------------------------
Native file protection can select Telegram and Signal together for the same user.
Fresh explicit configuration:
  sudo dlp-lab-configure --user YOUR_USER --telegram /path/to/Telegram --signal /opt/Signal/signal-desktop --enable
For an already configured package, stop its system service and use --replace
with BOTH executable paths; this backs up host settings and resets exclusions.
Omitting --signal on replacement removes Signal from the generated configuration.
First-install debconf still configures Telegram only; Signal is opt-in through
this administrative command. The parser rejects duplicate or identical paths.
Both process adapters must be enabled before the worker signals readiness.
Executable replacement requires restarting the service. Signal helpers running
a different executable and portal-mediated opens are not automatically covered.
A successful build is not proof of blocking Signal attachments: validate it with
synthetic files and confirmed DENY events on the deployment machine.

Operational fixes (0.2.4)
------------------------
Validated root-owned fontconfig cache files are allowed without content analysis.
Since 0.2.14 the private home .Xauthority file is also exempt after owner,
permissions, link-count and FD identity checks. Content at this exact path is
not inspected, including documents deliberately placed there.
The exception checks the exact directory, filename, ownership, permissions and
FD/inode identity; it does not exempt arbitrary files under /var/cache.
Desktop warnings request normal urgency and a 10-second expiry. The desktop
notification server controls the actual display duration.

Offline Signal messages (0.2.5)
------------------------------
The package now depends on libsqlcipher1, python3-pycryptodome, python3-gi and
 gir1.2-secret-1. Apt installs these dependencies; no bundled library is needed.
After fully quitting Signal, run as its desktop user, without sudo:

  dlp-agent signal-local-check --root "$HOME/.config/Signal" --journal "$HOME/dlp-signal-local.jsonl"

Only Linux GNOME libsecret and protected v11 keys are supported for now. The
user's existing Signal secret is requested through the session keyring; no
fallback to plaintext keys or backend changes. A locked keyring can prompt.
No key or original message is logged. A private temporary copy is checked
read-only, and the original is checked for changes. This is a cooperative
closed-client workflow, not protection against deliberate concurrent tampering.
The parent cleans up on worker timeout; killing the parent abruptly can leave
a temporary copy. The log is append-only JSONL at the chosen path, owned by the
user with mode 0600. It is not a tamper-resistant audit store.

Default sample: 100 recent nonempty bodies up to 16 KiB each. --limit accepts
1..1000; --no-notify disables only the desktop warning. The report distinguishes
oversized skipped bodies, incomplete analysis and additional rows. Attachment
contents and the full history semantics are not covered. BLOCK is retrospective
classification, not a sending veto. The command produces at most one warning
per run and does not deduplicate across runs.

This is an explicit session command, not an automatic systemd/keyring job. File
service auto-start and sudo dlp-lab-run-files remain unchanged. Existing host
settings are preserved on upgrade; do not rerun configure --replace just to
upgrade. An installed 0.2.4 must be upgraded before this command is available.

Central roles (0.2.6): opt-in dlp-lab-central.service and dlp-lab-forwarder.service,
plus user dlp-lab-forwarder-user.service. Manual dlp-agent central commands remain.
Server config: /etc/dlp-lab/central-server.json (host/port/cert/key); server state:
/var/lib/dlp-central, automatically initialized on first configured service start.
Agent config: /etc/dlp-lab/central-agent.json, root-owned 0600, passed with systemd
LoadCredential. System sender state: /var/lib/dlp-forwarder. Enable selected roles
explicitly with systemctl enable --now after configuration. No network roles are
enabled by package installation. Non-loopback servers require TLS.
The system sender runs as dlp-forwarder with systemd-journal supplementary group;
that group permits reading the whole journal, while code selects only DLP units.
User sender reads ~/.config/dlp-lab/central-agent.json and uses its own state.
Purge retains central credentials, audit databases and service accounts.
See repository docs/CENTRAL_SERVER.md for setup, migration and limitations.

Удобная первоначальная настройка (с версии 0.2.11)
-------------------------------------------------
sudo dlp-lab-configure --wizard

Мастер ищет Telegram в /usr/bin и /opt/Telegram, Signal в /opt/Signal,
позволяет указать другой путь и пользователя рабочего стола. Перед применением
показывает выбор. После подтверждения использует те же проверки root-owned
исполняемых файлов и атомарную запись, что обычный configure. Существующую
конфигурацию не заменяет. При отказе от запуска сохраняет настройки; вручную:
sudo systemctl start dlp-lab-files.service
Для запуска в переднем плане: sudo dlp-lab-run-files (сначала остановить службу).

Диагностика без sudo: dlp-agent doctor
Для обработки скриптом: dlp-agent doctor --json
Коды: 0 — нет обнаруженных ошибок; 1 — есть ошибки; 2 — systemd недоступен.
Нулевой код не гарантирует полную защиту. Проверяются пакетная политика,
пути мессенджеров и состояния system units. Токены и сообщения не читаются.
Сеть, сертификат, очередь и пользовательские модули этим отчётом не проверяются.
Мастер настраивает только файловый контроль. Подключение к серверу пока отдельное;
регистрация по одноразовому коду из панели в этой версии не реализована.

Docker central deployment (current lab, 2026-11-05)
------------------------------------------------
Agents remain DEB/systemd services. The migrated central server runs two Docker
containers, preserving loopback HTTP8766 and LAN HTTPS8767. Its root-owned Compose
configuration is /etc/dlp-lab/docker/compose.json (project dlp-central-migrated).
The database remains /var/lib/dlp-central, owned by UID10001. Do not start the old
central systemd units, run central init, or use sudo -u dlp-central on that database.
Updating this DEB does not update the Docker image. See docs/DOCKER.md for image
updates, registration, and backups. sudo dlp-admin-token still reads the same token.
New policy defaults enable ML WARN; preserve administrator conffiles on upgrade.
The last verified Debian12 package remains 0.2.14~deb12u1.
